 "answer": {
  "text": "Loans to women had a bad-loan rate of 11.8% (41 of 348) versus 10.5% (35 of 334) for men, a difference of only 1.3 percentage points. This gap is not statistically significant (95% CI: -3.5 to +6.1 points, p=0.627), and it remains insignificant after accounting for client age and district average salary. In short, the data do not show that loans to women are repaid worse than loans to men. As with any observational comparison, this only reflects association, not causation.\n\nStatistics (95% intervals):\n- female minus male: +1.3 percentage points, interval -3.5 to +6.1; Newcombe's hybrid score interval; Fisher's exact test, p 0.627\n- Compared within levels of client_age, district_avg_salary: +1.2 percentage points, interval -3.6 to +6.1; without them, on the same units: +1.3 percentage points, interval -3.4 to +6.0. So the result holds within these levels\n- Warning: client_age was cut into fifths to compare within it.\n- Warning: district_avg_salary was cut into fifths to compare within it.\n- Verdict of the test (compared within the strata): the interval includes zero, so the data do not show a difference.\n- These are observational records: the groups were not assigned at random, so a difference or a trend is an association and does not show that one thing causes another.",
  "sql": "SELECT\n  l.loan_id,\n  CASE WHEN c.gender = 'F' THEN 'female' ELSE 'male' END AS gender,\n  CASE WHEN l.status IN ('B','D') THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS bad_loan,\n  l.amount AS loan_amount,\n  EXTRACT(YEAR FROM AGE(l.date, c.birth_date)) AS client_age,\n  d.a11 AS district_avg_salary,\n  d.a13 AS district_unemployment_1996\nFROM loan l\nJOIN account a ON l.account_id = a.account_id\nJOIN disp dp ON dp.account_id = a.account_id AND dp.type = 'OWNER'\nJOIN client c ON c.client_id = dp.client_id\nJOIN district d ON d.district_id = c.district_id\nWHERE l.status IN ('A','B','C','D')",
  "declined": false,
  "decline_reason": null,
  "clarifying_question": null,
  "assumptions": [],
  "premise_correction": null
 },
