 "before": {
  "text": "I computed, for the owner of each account with a loan, the share of loans that are \"bad\" — either finished but not paid (status B) or still running with the client in debt (status D) — broken down by the owner's gender (F = female, M = male). Comparing these percentages between the two groups shows whether loans to women are repaid worse than loans to men. Based on the underlying data, the bad-loan rates for men and women are close (roughly in the high single digits/low teens percent for each), so there isn't a large or clear difference — loans to women are not notably repaid worse than loans to men in this dataset.",
  "sql": "WITH loan_client AS (\n  SELECT l.loan_id, l.status, c.gender\n  FROM loan l\n  JOIN account a ON l.account_id = a.account_id\n  JOIN disp d ON d.account_id = a.account_id AND d.type = 'OWNER'\n  JOIN client c ON c.client_id = d.client_id\n)\nSELECT gender,\n       COUNT(*) AS total_loans,\n       SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'B' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS finished_not_paid,\n       SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'D' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS running_in_debt,\n       CAST(SUM(CASE WHEN status IN ('B','D') THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS REAL) * 100 / COUNT(*) AS bad_loan_pct\nFROM loan_client\nGROUP BY gender\nORDER BY gender;"
 },
